              "C": "The peer group used as part of the relative valuation process may not be \nfairly valued.\nThe following information relates to questions \n6-8\nAn analyst gathers the following information for a set of firms from the same \nindustry.\nFirm\nP/E\nDividend payout ratio\nGrowth rate\nCost of equity\n1\n15.80\n80.00%\n2.0%\n8.2%\n2\n14.20\n75.00%\n2.5%\n8.0%\n3\n28.10\n70.00%\n5.0%\n8.3%\n4\n23.80\n72.00%\n4.0%\n7.5%\n5\n16.50\n82.00%\n2.5%\n7.5%\n6\n17.50\n90.00%\n2.0%\n7.8%\n7\n26.40\n77.00%\n4.0%\n8.0%\n8\n25.10\n81.00%\n3.5%\n7.8%\n9\n19.30\n70.00%\n3.5%\n7.0%\n10\n21.60\n79.00%\n3.0%\n8.5%\nThe average P/E of this peer group is 20.83, with a median of 20.45. Across the \ndistribution, the average payout ratio is 77.6%, the average growth rate is 3.2%, \nand the average cost of equity is 7.9%.\nThe analyst runs a linear regression of the P/E multiples on payout ratios and \ngrowth rates and the regression generates the following results.\nCoefficient\nStandard Error\nt-Stat.\nP-Value\nIntercept\n-32.63\n9.49\n-3.44\n0.0108\nPayout ratio\n42.54\n10.16\n4.19\n0.0041\nGrowth rate\n639.14\n64.50\n9.91\n0.0000\nThe analyst is curious about Firm 3's stock given the fact that its expected growth \nrate is the highest among the firms. However, she also notes that Firm 3's stock \ntrades at the highest P/E in the peer group. Additionally, she observes that \nFirm 7 offers higher payout to shareholders along with relatively high growth \nexpectations."
