            "solution_explanation": "The correct response is A: The fund's money-weighted rate of return will be less \nthan its time-weighted rate of return. Calculating the money-weighted return, \nrMV, requires finding the discount rate that sums the present value of cash flows \nto zero. Because most of the investment came during Year 2, the money-weighted \nreturn will be biased toward the performance of Year 2 when the return was low­\ner. The cash flows are as follows:\n\t\n■\nCF0 = −100\n\t\n■\nCF1 = −400\n\t\n■\nCF2 = [(100 × 1.14 × 1.08) + (400 × 1.08)] = 123.12 + 432.00 = 555.12.\nThe terminal value, here CF2, is determined by summing the investment returns \nfor each period.\n\tC F 0 +  \nC F 1 \n_ \n(1 + IRR) 1\n  +  \nC F 2 \n_ \n(1 + IRR) 2\n   =  − 100 +  − 400 \n_ \n(1 + IRR) 1\n  +  555.12 \n_ \n(1 + IRR) 2\n   =  0.\nThis results in a value of IRR = 9.05 percent.\nThis result is lower than the time-weighted return of:\n\tr\n TW   = \n2 √ _ \n(1.14)(1.08)  − 1 = 10.96%."
