            "solution_explanation": "The correct response is B. In a simple regression with a single binary- \nindicator-variable, the intercept represents the mean of the dependent variable \nwhen the indicator variable is zero. In this case, the indicator variable SHIFT \nequals zero in the years before the policy shift. Therefore, the intercept reflects \nthe mean annual growth rate in the money supply during the 15 years before the \npolicy shift.\nAlternative A is incorrect: The intercept does not represent the mean over the \nentire 30-year period; it only represents the mean when SHIFT = 0.\nAlternative C is incorrect: The intercept does not represent the mean in the 15 \nyears after the policy shift; instead, the mean after the policy shift is the sum of \nthe intercept and the slope coefficient."
